    "title": "Turkey Drawn into Somalia's Internal Conflicts, Risking Goodwill",
    "original_title": "Turkey, major power in Somalia, drawn into internal conflicts",
    "slug": "turkey-drawn-into-somalia-s-internal-conflicts-risking-goodwill",
    "summary": "Turkey's military involvement in Somalia's internal conflicts, including airstrikes in Baidoa and Doolow, risks undermining its longstanding support and goodwill in the country.",
    "content": "Turkey has become a major foreign power in Somalia, investing heavily in infrastructure, education, and military aid. However, its recent military actions have drawn it into Somalia's internal political and security conflicts, potentially jeopardizing its reputation as a neutral partner.\n\nOn Monday, Turkish F-16 fighter jets conducted airstrikes around Baidoa, a city in southern Somalia, to support federal troops against an attack by forces loyal to a former governor. Additionally, Turkey was accused of an airstrike on September 3 in Doolow that injured opposition leader General Mahad Abdirahman. The Somali government stated that the general was injured by explosives he was preparing, but the incident has raised questions about Turkey's role in internal disputes.\n\nThese events highlight Turkey's deepening military engagement in Somalia, which began with a strategic partnership in 2011. Since then, Turkey has built schools, hospitals, and roads, and trained Somali soldiers. However, as Somalia prepares for elections, political tensions are rising, and Turkey's support for the federal government may be perceived as taking sides.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this development is concerning because Turkey has been a reliable ally and a source of remittances and investment. Many diaspora members have invested in Turkish-built projects or rely on Turkish aid. If Turkey is seen as interfering in internal conflicts, it could strain community ties and reduce the neutral space that has benefited diaspora engagement.\n\nThe international community is watching closely, as Turkey's actions could set a precedent for other foreign powers involved in Somalia. The Somali government has not commented on the airstrikes, but opposition figures have condemned them. It remains to be seen whether Turkey can maintain its goodwill while navigating Somalia's complex political landscape.\n\nRead the original report from Somalia Guardian here: https://www.somaliguardian.com/news/somalia-news/turkey-major-power-somalia-drawn-into-internal-conflict/",
