    "title": "Drought Displaced Families from Lower Shabelle Crowd into Afgoye Camp with No Services",
    "original_title": "Drought-hit farming and herding families from villages in Lower Shabelle crowd into a camp in Afgoye",
    "slug": "drought-displaced-families-from-lower-shabelle-crowd-into-afgoye-camp-with-no-services",
    "summary": "Families from Basra, Alifow, and Baqdaad in Lower Shabelle have moved to Sooglow camp near Afgoye after poor rains destroyed crops and livestock. The camp lacks basic services, leaving residents like Hamdi Muhudin Ali and her 10 children struggling to survive.",
    "content": "Families from the villages of Basra, Alifow, and Baqdaad in Somalia's Lower Shabelle region have been arriving at Sooglow camp near Afgoye since August, following failed rains that devastated their crops and killed their livestock. The camp, which offers no basic services, has become a last resort for these pastoral and farming communities.\n\nAmong those displaced is Hamdi Muhudin Ali, a 51-year-old mother of 10 whose husband abandoned the family. She arrived at the camp with her children and an elderly relative. With no income and no assistance beyond sporadic handouts from local residents, the family often goes hungry. Her children travel into Afgoye town to beg, returning with a few dollars to buy food and firewood.\n\nThe drought in Lower Shabelle is part of a broader pattern of climate shocks that have repeatedly hit Somalia in recent years. Failed rainy seasons have left many rural families without livelihoods, forcing them to migrate to urban areas in search of aid. Afgoye, located about 30 kilometers northwest of Mogadishu, has seen an influx of such displaced people, straining already limited resources.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this crisis is a reminder of the ongoing fragility in their homeland. Many in the diaspora send remittances to relatives affected by drought, but the scale of need often outstrips private support. The lack of services at Sooglow camp highlights gaps in humanitarian response that diaspora organizations and international agencies are struggling to fill.\n\nThe situation is particularly dire for female-headed households like Hamdi's, who have few options for earning a living. With no schools or health facilities in the camp, children are at risk of missing education and facing health problems. Aid groups warn that without urgent intervention, conditions could worsen as the drought persists.\n\nThe Somali government and its partners have called for increased funding to address the humanitarian needs, but access to affected areas remains challenging due to security concerns. For now, families in Sooglow camp rely on the generosity of nearby residents and the resilience of their community.\n\nRead the original report from Radio Ergo here: https://radioergo.org/en/2026/10/drought-hit-farming-and-herding-families-from-villages-in-lower-shabelle-crowd-into-a-camp-in-afgoye/",
    "source": "radioergo.org",
    "source_name": "Radio Ergo",
    "original_url": "https://radioergo.org/en/2026/10/drought-hit-farming-and-herding-families-from-villages-in-lower-shabelle-crowd-into-a-camp-in-afgoye/",
    "country": "Somalia",
