    "title": "Ethiopia Plans Withdrawal of Over 3,000 Troops from Somalia",
    "original_title": "Itoobiya oo qorshaynaysa inay Soomaaliya kala Baxdo kumannaan Askari",
    "slug": "ethiopia-plans-withdrawal-of-over-3-000-troops-from-somalia",
    "summary": "Ethiopia is preparing to pull more than 3,000 soldiers out of Somalia to reinforce its own internal conflict, according to a Bloomberg report citing unnamed sources. The withdrawal could begin within days or weeks.",
    "content": "Ethiopia is planning to withdraw more than 3,000 of its troops stationed in Somalia, according to a Bloomberg report published on Wednesday. The report, which cited unnamed sources familiar with the matter, said Addis Ababa intends to redeploy the soldiers to Ethiopia to bolster its fight against increasingly strong insurgent groups within its borders. The sources spoke on condition of anonymity because they were not authorized to discuss the matter publicly.\n\nThe withdrawal could begin in the coming days or weeks, the report added. Ethiopia has maintained a significant military presence in Somalia for years, initially as part of the African Union Mission in Somalia (AMISOM) and later under the African Union Transition Mission in Somalia (ATMIS). Ethiopian forces have been key in operations against al-Shabaab and in securing parts of central and southern Somalia. The exact number of Ethiopian troops currently in Somalia has not been publicly disclosed, but estimates run into the thousands.\n\nThe decision to pull back troops comes amid a deteriorating security situation inside Ethiopia. Government forces have been battling the Fano militia in the Amhara region and the Oromo Liberation Army (OLA) in Oromia, among other armed groups. The conflicts have stretched Ethiopia's military resources, prompting the need for reinforcements. Prime Minister Abiy Ahmed's government has not commented on the reported withdrawal plan.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, the planned pullback raises concerns about a security vacuum in areas currently patrolled by Ethiopian forces. Many in the diaspora have family members in regions where Ethiopian troops are deployed, and a sudden departure could expose civilians to increased risk from al-Shabaab and other armed actors. The diaspora also closely follows developments in Somalia because of remittances and investments that depend on stability. A reduction in foreign troop presence could also affect the timeline for Somali security forces to take full responsibility for the country's security.\n\nEthiopia's withdrawal would mark a significant shift in the regional security landscape. It could also strain relations between Mogadishu and Addis Ababa, which have been tense over recent disputes, including the maritime deal between Ethiopia and Somaliland. Somali officials have previously warned that a hasty exit of Ethiopian forces could undermine gains made against al-Shabaab. The African Union and international partners have not yet responded to the Bloomberg report.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/10/01/itoobiya-oo-qorshaynaysa-inay-soomaaliya-kala-baxdo-kumannaan-askari/",
